PHASE 6 — EXISTING EMAIL ACCOUNT DUPLICATE PREVENTION
==================================================

THIS IS A CRITICAL REQUIREMENT.

The current system must correctly handle customers who already have an account.

Example:

A customer registers using:

Email: customer@example.com

Password: ********

The account is created successfully.

The customer logs out.

Later, the customer attempts to register again using the same email.

EXPECTED BEHAVIOR:

- Do not create a second account.
- Do not create duplicate database records.
- Do not return an unhandled server error.
- Do not crash the registration form.
- Do not create conflicting authentication sessions.

Provide a helpful registration response.

Suggested message:

"An account may already be associated with this email. Please sign in or reset your password."

Provide convenient actions:

- Sign In.
- Forgot Password.

If the existing account is unverified, follow the existing email verification workflow and provide an appropriate resend-verification option.

IMPORTANT:

The user must never need to create another account just because they logged out.

They should use the existing Sign In system.

Normalize email addresses consistently before checking uniqueness.

Handle case differences and surrounding whitespace.

Example:

Customer@Example.com

customer@example.com

These must be treated as the same normalized email identity.

Enforce uniqueness at the database level, not just in the frontend.

Handle database duplicate-key errors gracefully.

Use appropriate rate limiting to protect registration and account-enumeration-sensitive endpoints.

